# Protected TLA+ contracts require explicit human review.
/spec/tla/protected/ @husams
/spec/tla/trusted/ @husams
/spec/tla/modules/CidxTypes.tla @husams
/spec/tla/models/*.cfg @husams
# HSE-89: TLAPS proofs and exported counterexamples are trust artifacts --
# an AI agent must not weaken a proof obligation or replace a golden
# counterexample to make a gate green.
/spec/tla/proofs/ @husams
/spec/tla/counterexamples/golden/ @husams
# HSE-89 review fix: the policy files that DEFINE what is protected and who
# owns it must themselves be protected, or a change could edit them to
# silently narrow protectedPaths or replace the owner before the same PR
# merges. check-protected-review.sh resolves both files from the base commit
# specifically so this cannot be bypassed by editing them at HEAD.
/.github/CODEOWNERS @husams
/spec/tla/manifest.json @husams
# HSE-89 review round 2: the scripts that ENFORCE this policy are themselves
# protected. verification.yml also executes check-protected-review.sh from
# the base commit rather than the PR's own checkout, so this file's edits
# cannot make that one gate evaluate itself against its own tampered copy;
# the other two scripts here rely on this CODEOWNERS entry as their
# protection since they are not similarly pinned-to-base.
/spec/tla/tools/check-protected-review.sh @husams
/spec/tla/tools/check-policy.sh @husams
/spec/tla/tools/select-changed-gates.sh @husams
# HSE-89 P1 fix (internal critic): the gate-selection defense had the same
# self-policing gap check-protected-review.sh was already fixed for -- a PR
# could narrow ci-dependency-map.json's flow->gates mapping and delete the
# matching assertion in check-gate-selection.sh in the same change. Both
# files are now protected, and verification.yml executes check-gate-selection.sh
# from the base commit (mirroring check-protected-review.sh) so the base's
# unedited assertions are what actually validate the PR's (head) map.
/spec/tla/tools/check-gate-selection.sh @husams
/spec/tla/tools/check-gate-selection-defense-regression.sh @husams
/spec/tla/tools/validate-manifest.py @husams
/spec/tla/ci-dependency-map.json @husams
# HSE-89 internal-critic P1 (round 5): the scripts that ENFORCE the actual
# formal-verification results -- TLC model checking, TLAPS proofs,
# conformance/sidecar-conformance replay, and the counterexample-export
# pipeline -- were themselves unprotected, even though the meta-scripts that
# police gate SELECTION and review APPROVAL were already fixed in prior
# rounds. A PR could weaken a protected invariant and replace one of these
# with a stub that exits 0 in the same commit. check.sh,
# check-conformance.sh, check-sidecar-conformance.sh, and check-proofs.sh are
# additionally pinned-to-base in verification.yml (mirroring
# check-protected-review.sh and check-gate-selection.sh). check-regression.sh
# and export-counterexample.sh are self-tests of those pinned checkers and
# had the same two-generation trust-root-poisoning gap check-gate-selection-
# defense-regression.sh was already fixed for: a single PR could weaken
# check.sh's detection and neuter these self-tests' assertions in the same
# commit, so every gate would still go green. They are now ALSO pinned-to-
# base in verification.yml, and their CIDX_CHECK_SH wiring points them at the
# SAME base-extracted check.sh as the real gate, from the same immutable
# base revision -- see check-self-test-tamper-regression.sh for the
# executable proof. CODEOWNERS remains a defense-in-depth backstop for both.
/spec/tla/tools/check.sh @husams
/spec/tla/tools/check-regression.sh @husams
/spec/tla/tools/check-conformance.sh @husams
/spec/tla/tools/check-sidecar-conformance.sh @husams
/spec/tla/tools/check-proofs.sh @husams
/spec/tla/tools/export-counterexample.sh @husams
/spec/tla/tools/check-verification-tamper-regression.sh @husams
/spec/tla/tools/check-self-test-tamper-regression.sh @husams
# HSE-89 round-3 acceptance-review finding: check-proofs-vacuous-comment-
# regression.sh is check-proofs.sh's self-test, the same role
# check-regression.sh plays for check.sh -- and check-regression.sh is listed
# here. This entry closes the same coverage-consistency gap for the newer
# file; it was already base-extracted in verification.yml (mirroring
# check-regression.sh) and covered by check-verification-tamper-regression.sh's
# checker table, so this is defense-in-depth, not a new protection mechanism.
/spec/tla/tools/check-proofs-vacuous-comment-regression.sh @husams
# HSE-89 round-8 (QA + senior-developer round-2 acceptance review):
# check-proofs.sh's theorem/invariant structural-binding logic was factored
# out into this sibling file so it is directly unit-testable without a real
# tlapm run; it carries the identical sensitivity as check-proofs.sh itself
# (a weakened spec-operator resolution here reintroduces the vacuous-proof
# bypass that check-proofs-vacuous-comment-regression.sh's fifth seed
# pins), so it is base-pinned in verification.yml alongside check-proofs.sh
# and protected here too. check-proofs-binding-unit-test.sh is its
# tlapm-free self-test, the same role check-regression.sh plays for check.sh.
/spec/tla/tools/check-proofs-binding.sh @husams
/spec/tla/tools/check-proofs-binding-unit-test.sh @husams
# HSE-89 round-6 security fix: extract-trusted-checker.sh is the single
# shared implementation every base-pinning extraction step in
# verification.yml now calls (except check-protected-review.sh, which stays
# unconditional and hand-written on purpose -- see its own workflow
# comment). It decides, for every OTHER pinned checker, whether the base
# commit resolves at all and whether a missing path is genuine
# first-introduction versus an unresolvable ref; a PR that could edit it
# unreviewed could make every one of those checkers' bootstrap fallbacks
# degrade silently to head. It is itself always invoked straight from head
# (there is no lower layer left to extract it from), so CODEOWNERS review is
# its only protection, same as select-changed-gates.sh below.
/spec/tla/tools/extract-trusted-checker.sh @husams
# HSE-89 critic finding: the workflow file itself was unprotected -- a PR
# could remove or defang the tla-protected-diff-review job (or what it
# extracts/executes) and nothing would flag it as needing review. Known
# residual limitation, not fixable in-repo: at the base commit for the PR
# that FIRST adds these protectedPaths/CODEOWNERS entries, none of them
# exist yet, so this and the other HSE-89 self-protection entries only take
# effect for PRs based on a commit at or after this one merges.
/.github/workflows/verification.yml @husams
